{P} ... so I think that the problem is due to something going wrong in my
{P} system that corrupted the save file. However I don't know what's wrong
{P} and where...

Well NetHack "corrupted" the save file.  That is to say, not enough info was
saved in the old save file to restore properly in the new memory setup.
So old save file was "correct", it was read properly, restored to
what you would view as "incorrect" setup, and then (if you saved again
(which you probably did)) was saved as an "incorrect" setup.

{P} I'd like to know a couple of things:
{P}  does someone know what can cause this problem?

Yes.  It has to do with the fact that nethack saves a pointer to a memory
location of the object names (changed for 3.1 :-).  For some versions of
the game (most notably the MSDOS PC version) the pointer written to the
file was too short and the restore calculations yeild an invalid number.

So, if you have say 594321 bytes free when you run and save one game of
NetHack, and then you restore with anything but 594321 bytes free you
will see this problem.

Later released versions of the binary (there were 3 releases you need the
3rd) fixed this trouble.  Get it from linc.cis.upenn.edu or
comp.binaries.ibm.pc archives.  Also, a code patch was posted here to r.g.h.

{P}  does someone know how can I fix it and get my game back?

Yes and no.

To fix: Get the new binary.  Your old save file is pretty much garbage
by now though.

To get old game back: You have few choices.
	1) If you just happen to save your old *.SAV files, then put your
	   PC back to however it used to be and restore your game.
	   That's the easy part "fix".

	2) If all you got is the new and screwed up save file then you
	   might attempt some stuff.
	   No guarentees here.  I've never tried this myself.

	   a) make a copy of your save file (you will probably need to
	      try this several times to get this correct).

	   b) Note your current memory configuration (in  # of bytes).
	      Let's say for example it's 594321 bytes free.

	   c) Restore under old memory configuration.  This should be
	      something like 594326 bytes free (+5 bytes).  Look and see
	      if names are correct.
	      For some reason I have a gut feeling that this might not
	      work though.

	   d) Try all the combinations of +5, +10, -5, and -10 bytes
	      from current memory available.  One of them might work.
	      How you get to those values is your own trouble though.
	      The only program I know (eatmem) goes by K not bytes.

Good luck.

						- Norm.

P.S.  I think the memory fix/change makes your save file incompatable with
